{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Such as has never been.</b> We find a masculine form for a feminine matter, and he raised from it in its construct state: כל אשר יתן ממנו לה׳ יהיה קדש (all that is given from it to HaShem shall be holy). And another interpretation: masculine and feminine — אל המחנה האחת והכהו והנה רוח גדולה וחזק (to the one camp and strike it, and behold a great and strong wind). And one may say that <b>כמהו</b> does not refer to the cry but to the plague, as it is written above: כחצות הלילה אני יוצא (at midnight I go out). And this is what it says: the like of that night — such a cry has never been and shall never again be."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The first scriptural citation appears to be from Leviticus 27 (v. 9 or similar), raised as evidence of masculine form used for feminine referent; the second citation is from 1 Kings 19:11. The word בקומצו is unclear; rendered here as 'in its construct state' (from קמץ/קמוץ) but the term is unusual and may reflect a textual difficulty."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:00.856833820Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}